`,s.jsx(q,{items:[{q:"Why is a point on a long straight edge a bad feature?",a:"Because you cannot localise it along the edge. Sliding the window parallel to the edge produces an identical patch, so any position along it matches equally well — the aperture problem. One degree of freedom is unconstrained, so the match is ambiguous. A corner constrains both."},{q:"State the Harris response and what each sign means.",a:"R = det(M) - k*trace(M)^2, where M is the windowed structure tensor of the image gradients. R near zero means flat (both eigenvalues small), R large and negative means edge (one eigenvalue dominates), R large and positive means corner (both eigenvalues large). Using det and trace avoids ever computing the eigenvalues."},{q:"Harris is invariant to rotation but not to scale. Why the asymmetry?",a:"Rotation conjugates the structure tensor by a rotation matrix, which preserves its eigenvalues, so R is unchanged. Scale changes the relationship between the fixed window size and the image structure: zoom in far enough and a corner no longer fits in the window, so it reads as a smooth edge. Fixing that requires searching over scales, which is what SIFT's difference-of-Gaussian pyramid does."},{q:"What is the difference between a detector and a descriptor, and which is HOG?",a:"A detector answers 'where are the interesting points'; a descriptor answers 'what does the neighbourhood of this point look like, in a form I can compare'. HOG is a descriptor only — it is computed densely on a fixed grid rather than at detected keypoints, which is why it pairs with a sliding window rather than with matching."}]}),`
